<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  CardDemo  -  Structured Logging Configuration  (logback-spring.xml)
  ============================================================================
  Purpose
  Structured-logging configuration for the migrated CardDemo application. It
  satisfies the Observability rule (Technical Specification sec. 0.8.2, 0.9.5):
  "structured logging carrying correlation IDs ... verified to work in the
  local development environment."

  Why the "-spring" filename
  The "logback-spring.xml" name (NOT "logback.xml") lets Spring Boot process
  the <springProfile> and <springProperty> extension tags. This is what allows
  the configuration to differ per profile (human-readable console locally,
  structured JSON otherwise) and to read Spring Environment properties such as
  spring.application.name.

  Correlation / trace-context model  (spec sec. 0.4.1, 0.5.5, 0.7.1)
  Three MDC keys are surfaced on EVERY log line so that a single request or
  batch job can be followed end-to-end:
    * traceId       - injected automatically by Micrometer Tracing
                      (micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel) into the SLF4J MDC.
    * spanId        - injected automatically by Micrometer Tracing.
    * correlationId - a request/job identifier placed into the MDC by
                      com.aws.carddemo.observability.CorrelationIdFilter (web)
                      and by a batch job/step listener (see Batch note below).
  Each key is rendered with the MDC default-value operator "%X{key:-}", which
  prints an empty value (rather than the literal text "key_IS_UNDEFINED") when
  the key is absent - e.g. for framework log lines emitted outside any request
  or job.

  NOTE: Spring Boot's imported defaults.xml also registers a "correlationId"
  CONVERSION WORD (the CorrelationIdConverter, which renders trace+span). That
  is deliberately NOT used here; the "%X{correlationId:-}" MDC-key form below
  refers to the application's own per-request/per-job correlation identifier,
  which is a distinct value. There is no collision between the two.

  Dependencies
  ZERO extra logging dependencies. Logback ships transitively with
  spring-boot-starter, and the structured (JSON) encoder used below is Spring
  Boot's own StructuredLogEncoder (available since Spring Boot 3.4, present in
  the pinned 3.5.16). No logstash-logback-encoder or any other third-party JSON
  encoder is required or added.

  ============================================================================
  SECURITY  -  NEVER LOG SENSITIVE VALUES  (spec sec. 0.7.3 "L1", 0.9.3)
  ============================================================================
  The card CVV (COBOL field CARD-CVV-CD) and any password (SEC-USR-PWD) MUST
  NEVER be written to a log or placed into the MDC. The authoritative control
  is code-side omission: mappers/services never log these fields and DTOs
  exclude them from toString(); the CVV is never returned in full. This
  configuration file introduces nothing that could expose them, and it must
  never be changed to do so. Two concrete safeguards are enforced here:

    1. Hibernate/JPA bind-parameter TRACE logging is left OFF (default). The
       loggers "org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind" and
       "org.hibernate.type.descriptor.sql" are intentionally NOT enabled at
       TRACE/DEBUG, because bound SQL parameters can contain sensitive column
       values (CVV, password).

    2. Defense-in-depth masking (see CARDDEMO_CONSOLE_LOG_PATTERN below): the
       human-readable message is passed through a Logback replace(...) that
       redacts anything shaped like "cvv=", "password=", "pwd=" tokens. This is
       a secondary net; it is NOT a substitute for code-side omission. For the
       structured (JSON) profile the encoder builds output from the event and
       MDC directly (not from a pattern), so masking there is achieved by the
       same code-side omission plus never putting secrets in the MDC; a
       Spring Boot StructuredLoggingJsonMembersCustomizer bean is the supported
       place to register field-level masking should it ever become necessary.

  Lifecycle note: "scan" is intentionally omitted. Spring Boot owns the
  logback-spring.xml lifecycle, and periodic scanning re-evaluates
  <springProfile> without a guaranteed Environment, so it is avoided here.
-->
<configuration>

    <!-- Inherit Spring Boot's conversion rules (clr, correlationId, wEx, ...),
         the CONSOLE_LOG_* / FILE_LOG_* properties (charset, threshold), and the
         sensible default levels for noisy third-party loggers. -->
    <include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/defaults.xml"/>

    <!-- Service name for the log context; read from the Spring Environment and
         defaulted to "carddemo" when spring.application.name is unset. -->
    <springProperty scope="context" name="appName"
                    source="spring.application.name" defaultValue="carddemo"/>

    <!-- Human-readable console pattern (used by the "local" profile).
         It appends the full trace context to Spring Boot's standard layout:
           [appName] [traceId,spanId] [cid=correlationId]
         The message itself is wrapped in a lightweight, comma-free Logback
         replace(...) that redacts cvv/password/pwd tokens as defense-in-depth
         (the primary control is code-side omission - see the SECURITY block).
         The regex intentionally contains NO comma, because Logback's replace
         converter splits its {regex, replacement} options on the first comma. -->
    <property name="CARDDEMO_CONSOLE_LOG_PATTERN"
              value="%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSS} %-5level [${appName}] [%X{traceId:-},%X{spanId:-}] [cid=%X{correlationId:-}] %logger{36} - %replace(%msg){'(?i)(cvv|password|passwd|pwd)(\s*[=:]\s*)(\S+)', '$1$2***REDACTED***'}%n"/>

    <!-- ==========================  local profile  ==========================
         Developer-facing, human-readable console output. The local profile may
         raise application verbosity to DEBUG via application-local.yml
         (logging.level.com.aws.carddemo: DEBUG), which overrides the levels
         declared in this file; DEBUG is therefore NOT hardcoded here. -->
    <springProfile name="local">
        <appender name="CONSOLE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
            <filter class="ch.qos.logback.classic.filter.ThresholdFilter">
                <level>${CONSOLE_LOG_THRESHOLD}</level>
            </filter>
            <encoder>
                <pattern>${CARDDEMO_CONSOLE_LOG_PATTERN}</pattern>
                <charset>${CONSOLE_LOG_CHARSET}</charset>
            </encoder>
        </appender>
        <root level="INFO">
            <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
        </root>
    </springProfile>

    <!-- ========================  non-local profiles  =======================
         Everything that is not "local" (test, dev, staging, prod, or no active
         profile) emits structured JSON via Spring Boot's framework-native
         StructuredLogEncoder using the Elastic Common Schema (ECS) format. The
         ECS encoder automatically includes all MDC key/value pairs, so
         traceId, spanId and correlationId appear as first-class JSON fields -
         no extra dependency and no custom encoder required. -->
    <springProfile name="!local">
        <appender name="STRUCTURED_CONSOLE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
            <filter class="ch.qos.logback.classic.filter.ThresholdFilter">
                <level>${CONSOLE_LOG_THRESHOLD}</level>
            </filter>
            <encoder class="org.springframework.boot.logging.logback.StructuredLogEncoder">
                <format>ecs</format>
                <charset>${CONSOLE_LOG_CHARSET}</charset>
            </encoder>
        </appender>
        <root level="INFO">
            <appender-ref ref="STRUCTURED_CONSOLE"/>
        </root>
    </springProfile>

    <!-- Application logger level. Kept at INFO for parity with the root level;
         profiles override it through application(-<profile>).yml as noted
         above. (Do NOT hardcode DEBUG here.) -->
    <logger name="com.aws.carddemo" level="INFO"/>

    <!-- ============================  Batch note  ============================
         Correlation ID propagates across BOTH service AND batch boundaries
         (spec sec. 0.9.5). For web traffic the correlationId MDC value is set
         by com.aws.carddemo.observability.CorrelationIdFilter; for Spring Batch
         a job/step execution listener under com.aws.carddemo sets the same
         correlationId MDC key per job execution (and clears it afterwards).
         No appender-specific change is needed for batch: the console pattern
         already prints "%X{correlationId:-}" and the ECS encoder already emits
         the correlationId MDC field, so batch logs carry the correlation ID
         exactly like web-request logs. -->

</configuration>
